WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.133
- [Narrator] J. Cole released

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:07.158
the much-anticipated music video

00:00:07.166 --> 00:00:08.616
to his song, "Middle Child."

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.470
It was directed by Mez,

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:12.699
and Scott Lazer was the creative director.

00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:15.711
Lazer also directed a
number of Cole's videos

00:00:15.711 --> 00:00:19.690
including "ATM," "Kevin's
Heart," "4 Your Eyez Only,"

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.900
and his documentary and concert series,

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.240
"Forest Hills Drive Homecoming."

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.912
But what sets this project apart

00:00:25.912 --> 00:00:29.210
is how well the visuals
complement the overall message

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:30.400
of his lyrics.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.240
The video shows that while
Cole is continuously overlooked

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.330
in the music industry, the
rapper is still a quiet

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:38.100
but deadly hunter.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.600
Here's a breakdown of all the
things you might have missed

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.500
in J. Cole's newest video.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.810
♪ I'm all in my bag, this hard as it get ♪

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.140
- Throughout most of the video,

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.050
J. Cole is in the middle of the screen

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.280
while everything is happening around him.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.445
Sort of like how kids
describe their experiences

00:00:52.445 --> 00:00:54.539
being a middle child in their family.

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:56.870
The video also wastes little time

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.563
highlighting J. Cole's targets.

00:00:58.563 --> 00:01:01.300
The scene opens with Cole
in the center of the screen

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.034
with a silhouetted crowd behind him.

00:01:03.034 --> 00:01:04.920
When the beat drops,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.662
affluent figures on the
red carpet celebrate

00:01:06.662 --> 00:01:10.147
but aren't paying any
attention to Cole's presence.

00:01:10.147 --> 00:01:13.414
The red-carpet event is also in the woods.

00:01:13.414 --> 00:01:16.054
It's the first visual
hint of the hunting motif

00:01:16.054 --> 00:01:18.232
that comes up throughout the entire video.

00:01:18.232 --> 00:01:20.978
When the lights fade
and the scene changes,

00:01:20.978 --> 00:01:24.440
the lively crowd now
lies dead in the morgue.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.349
The red carpet has been
replaced with red dirt,

00:01:26.349 --> 00:01:29.550
which also covers the
soles of the dead's shoes.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:31.740
The red bottoms remind viewers

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.570
of the ever-popular
high-end Louboutin shoes

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.920
that have quickly become a status symbol.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.495
But the fact that the
soles are made from dirt

00:01:39.495 --> 00:01:42.770
make the wearer seem fake and ingenuine.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.040
The ironic tattoo that reads "loyalty"

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.650
on the female cadaver's ankle

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.490
also brings his message forward.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.168
But why this particular imagery?

00:01:51.168 --> 00:01:52.099
- What was the reason?
- I have a reason.

00:01:52.099 --> 00:01:53.429
- What was the reason?
- I have a reason.

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:55.340
- What was the reason?!
- I just explained!

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.190
- [Narrator] Well, when
it comes to award shows,

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.449
J. Cole has notoriously been snubbed.

00:01:59.449 --> 00:02:01.955
The artist has been up
for a music-related award

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:05.040
43 times since 2012

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:08.650
and only won six of them.
For the Grammys specifically,

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.460
the artist has been nominated seven times

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.560
and hasn't won once!

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.545
He's been beat out by
Childish Gambino, Ella Mai,

00:02:16.545 --> 00:02:19.735
Chance The Rapper, Kendrick
Lamar, and The Weeknd.

00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:24.670
But despite this, Cole has
been and still is killing it.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.440
The artist, who also produces
almost all of his records,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.810
went certified platinum with no features

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.500
with his 2014 album "Forest Hills Drive."

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:37.360
His albums "Cole World," "Born
Sinner," "4 Your Eyez Only,"

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:39.873
and "KOD" also went platinum.

00:02:39.873 --> 00:02:41.720
Cole continues riding around

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.820
in the familiar red dirt
backwoods in a Bentley.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.330
Covering the very expensive vehicle in mud

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.910
without a care in the world.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.930
We get a peek into his hunting lodge

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.818
with three human heads on
display over a fireplace.

00:02:53.818 --> 00:02:56.814
The plaques under the heads
read "Your Favorite Rapper,"

00:02:56.814 --> 00:02:59.838
"This Could Be You,"
and "Ask For A Feature."

00:02:59.838 --> 00:03:02.240
While the messages are pretty generic

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.560
and could fit a number
of overrated artists,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.838
people on Twitter are
giving their best guesses.

00:03:06.838 --> 00:03:09.110
My guess would have to be Lil Pump

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.620
for the artist in the middle.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.348
Given the colorful hair and
Cole and Pump's past beef.

00:03:13.348 --> 00:03:16.008
Last year, fans thought
Cole was calling out Pump

00:03:16.008 --> 00:03:18.654
in his "KOD" track "1985,"

00:03:18.654 --> 00:03:20.960
and tensions were high.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.493
Yes, the beef was squashed,

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:24.326
but the "This Could Be You" plaque

00:03:24.326 --> 00:03:25.810
could be a warning.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.910
Just a theory.

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.910
Another theory is that the colorful hair

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:31.464
just represents new age
rappers as a monolith.

00:03:31.464 --> 00:03:33.470
Cole's made it clear in his song

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:35.627
that he's apparently
over any personal beefs.

00:03:35.627 --> 00:03:37.762
♪ But I'd never beef with
a n---- for nothin' ♪

00:03:37.762 --> 00:03:39.600
♪ If I smoke a rapper,
it's gon' be legit ♪

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.910
- The lyrics overtly
reveal the meaning behind

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.429
the "Middle Child" song title.

00:03:43.429 --> 00:03:46.700
The artist feels that he
rests between two ages of rap:

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.451
old school and new school

00:03:48.451 --> 00:03:51.114
and is OK with acting as
a bridge between them.

00:03:51.114 --> 00:03:53.229
♪ I'm dead in the middle
of two generations ♪

00:03:53.229 --> 00:03:55.400
♪ I'm little bro and big bro all at once ♪

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.170
- He acknowledges that he learned a lot

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.480
from the legends before him

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.244
and is looking to help the
next wave of real artists.

00:04:01.244 --> 00:04:04.272
And the theme that sticks
out in this part of the video

00:04:04.272 --> 00:04:06.478
is what it means to be real.

00:04:06.478 --> 00:04:09.780
We've switched scenes from the
backwoods to the supermarket,

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.731
a play on the hunters versus the buyers.

00:04:11.731 --> 00:04:14.510
The buyers here are the
people who weren't out there

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:16.110
hunting for their success

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.340
but passively relying on
the innovation of others.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.720
Like this woman in camouflage

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.821
who's literally shopping for the juice.

00:04:22.821 --> 00:04:26.059
The supermarket environment
is also interesting

00:04:26.059 --> 00:04:28.490
in that it is the complete opposite

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.925
of the grungy outdoor
environment we just saw.

00:04:30.925 --> 00:04:33.970
Cole literally rolls
past and props himself

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.180
next to the cleaning product aisle.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.939
Notice again that Cole isn't
walking around the supermarket

00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:41.500
but is either sitting in the shopping cart

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:43.700
or propped up against the shelves.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.170
His art is what other
artists are trying to cop

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:47.600
for themselves.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.790
Another theme worth
noting is how black women

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:51.550
are portrayed throughout this video.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.790
Cole has a powerful, young,
all-female marching band

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:57.298
setting the pace and the
beat for the whole song.

00:04:57.298 --> 00:05:00.010
The idea of setting the pace is continued

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.280
with the shocking end to the video.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.080
A white woman notices a
black woman's babyhairs

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:07.764
and is immediately seen shopping
for a black woman's face

00:05:07.764 --> 00:05:09.630
in the grocery store.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.351
The black face had a red "special"
sticker on the packaging.

00:05:12.351 --> 00:05:14.079
J. Cole said it best:

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:16.570
♪ Money in your palm don't make you real ♪

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:18.581
- The video shows the
dangers of appropriation.

00:05:18.581 --> 00:05:21.064
How features and styles of
black women are only celebrated

00:05:21.064 --> 00:05:23.913
when they aren't on black women.

00:05:23.913 --> 00:05:27.140
Do you think the heads
on the mantles were shots

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:28.560
at specific artists?

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.143
Do you think Cole is way
overdue for a Grammy?

00:05:31.143 --> 00:05:33.000
Let us know in the comments below

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:34.500
and don't forget to subscribe.

